  <section id="about">
    <h2>About this page</h2>
    <p>
      Two pages on this site say Amin Samkough held a post in the “Caucasus
      Mountaineers’ People’s Party”: the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/circassian-center-emin-semsug/">Circassian Center profile</a>
      and the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/north-caucasians-in-emigration/">2020 dictionary entry</a>.
      This page collects what I could find about the party itself, and the
      sources to check next. It is a reading list with notes, not a translation.
      I found these sources by web search, and I have not read all of them in
      full. The reliability notes are my own judgment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: <strong>I found no independent source that names Samkough as a
        member.</strong> Both claims above are unsourced, and they may copy one
        another. The party’s existence, leader, dates and Syrian presence are
        well documented. His role in it is not.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="the-party">
    <h2>The party in brief</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Names</dt>
      <dd>
        <span lang="ru">Народная партия горцев Кавказа</span> (НПГК, NPGK);
        <span lang="tr">Kafkasya Dağlıları Halk Partisi</span> (KDHP); in
        English “People’s Party of the Caucasian Mountaineers (Highlanders)”.
        Its French organ was titled <em>Les Montagnards du Caucase</em>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Predecessor</dt>
      <dd>
        <span lang="ru">Народная партия вольных горцев Кавказа</span>
        (People’s Party of the Free Mountaineers), founded in Prague as the
        political wing of the Union of Mountaineers of the Caucasus. Its
        organ, <span lang="ru">«Вольные горцы»</span>, appeared in Prague in
        1927–28.
      </dd>
      <dt>Founded</dt>
      <dd>
        18 November 1926 in most sources. The place is disputed: Prague
        (Babich) or Warsaw (the historycaucasus.com blog).
      </dd>
      <dt>Leader</dt>
      <dd>
        Muhammad Said Shamil, general secretary; a Turkish citizen, born in
        Istanbul in 1901, and a grandson of Imam Shamil.
      </dd>
      <dt>Other figures named</dt>
      <dd>
        Lazar Bicherakhov (head of the European organization), Barasbi
        Baytugan, Balo Bilatti, T. Elekhoti, Prince Elmurza Bekovich-Cherkassky
        (first editor of the organ) and Zhanbek Khavzhoko.
      </dd>
      <dt>Backing</dt>
      <dd>
        Poland, through Tadeusz Schaetzel and with Piłsudski’s support, as part
        of the Polish-sponsored Prometheus movement.
      </dd>
      <dt>Aims</dt>
      <dd>
        Pan-Caucasian unity and anti-communism: a restored mountaineer republic
        inside a Caucasian Confederation, in the line of the 1917–1920
        Mountaineers’ Republic.
      </dd>
      <dt>Branches</dt>
      <dd>
        Prague, Brno, Paris, Istanbul and Warsaw. Babich also lists “Kunejt
        (Syria)” and Damascus among the places members came from. Jineps says
        affiliated associations were set up in Syria in 1928, Transjordan in
        1932 and Egypt in 1932.
      </dd>
      <dt>Publications</dt>
      <dd>
        <span lang="ru">«Горцы Кавказа»</span> /
        <span lang="tr">Kafkasya Dağlıları</span> (Paris and Warsaw, 1928–34,
        nos. 1–50) and <span lang="ru">«Северный Кавказ»</span> /
        <span lang="tr">Şimali Kafkasya</span> (Warsaw and Paris, 1934–39),
        plus several short-lived periodicals.
      </dd>
      <dt>Rivals</dt>
      <dd>
        Haydar Bammat’s Nationalist Democratic Party. In 1929 a split led by
        Ali Khan Kantemir produced a Federalist party in Istanbul.
      </dd>
      <dt>Active</dt>
      <dd>
        1926–1939, in four phases (1926–30, 1931–33, 1934–36, 1937–39). It
        stopped at the start of World War II. Babich judges that it had little
        success or prestige among émigrés but was an important stage in their
        political self-awareness.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: the founding place (Prague or Warsaw) and the founding year
        (1925 on Russian Wikipedia) differ between sources. The Jineps article
        says Czechoslovakia and then Poland, and the dictionary entry on this
        site is said to place the party in Paris and Istanbul too. These may be
        phases or offices of one body, or one source may be wrong.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="syria">
    <h2>The Syria link</h2>
    <p>
      The strongest hint is in Babich’s 2024 article on the Turkish vector. It
      lists “Kunejt (Syria)” and Damascus among the places party members came
      from, which fits Quneitra and Damascus. It also quotes Bicherakhov’s 1929
      memo saying a Bolshevik agent, Aydebulov, was sent to Syria to turn local
      Circassians against the party. So the party was active enough in Syria to
      be a target. The article does not name Samkough.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: that is still not evidence about him. The 1936 clash and his
        resignation as mayor, from Mirzoev’s account, show a Syria-based
        Circassian nationalist, but Mirzoev’s study never mentions the party.
        The “board member” wording comes only from the dictionary entry, which
        gives no source.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
